     "page": 1,
     "text": "CLINICAL SUMMARY - Wilson Turner and Gallagher Clinic\nPatient: Casey Wise DOB: 05/05/1952 Sex: M MRN: 742567\n\nEncounters:\n 2025-01-21 GERD BP 144/88 Wt 210\n 2024-06-17 kidney stones BP 155/83 Wt 208\n 2023-11-11 Proteinuria BP 142/86 Wt 206\n 2023-04-06 lab review BP 153/92 Wt 218\n 2022-08-31 back strain BP 145/82 Wt 203\n 2022-01-24 back strain BP 147/89 Wt 202\n 2021-06-19 medication refill BP 144/81 Wt 216\n 2020-11-13 Proteinuria BP 144/88 Wt 213\n\nAssessment/Plan:\n • Proteinuria (urine protein 98 mg/dL) - currently under control, no medication required. Follow-up urinalysis scheduled in 6 months.\n • GERD - patient reports no recent symptoms of heartburn or acid reflux. Continues on omeprazole 20 mg famotidine 40 mg as prescribed. Recommend continuing the current regimen and revisiting in 3 months.\n • Kidney stones - Mr. Wise reports no new episodes of renal colic since his last visit. He is compliant with tamsulosin 0.4 mg. Advised to maintain a high fluid intake and a diet low in oxalate. Annual follow-up recommended.\n\nVitals - BP: 144/88, 155/83, 142/86, 153/92, 145/82, 147/89, 144/81, 144/88\n - Weight: 210, 208, 206, 218, 203, 202, 216, 213\n\nAdditional Clinical Information:\nMr. Wise is a 72-year-old male with a history of proteinuria, GERD, and kidney stones. His proteinuria, measured at 98 mg/dL, is currently well-controlled with no additional medications. His GERD symptoms have been effectively managed with a combination of omeprazole 20 mg and famotidine 40 mg. For his kidney stones, tamsulosin 0.4 mg has been prescribed, and he has not experienced any new episodes since the last visit. \n\nSocial History:\nMr. Wise has a history of tobacco use, having smoked half a pack per day since 1976. He quit on February 27, 2023. Alcohol consumption is approximately three drinks per week. His mother had type 2 diabetes, which was diagnosed at age 76.\n\nExamination Findings:\nGeneral: Well-appearing male in no acute No abnormalities.\nCardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs.\nRespiratory: Clear to auscultation bilaterally.\nAbdomen: Soft, non-tender, no organomegaly.\nGenitourinary: No CVA tenderness, normal external genitalia.\nMusculoskeletal: No deformities, full range of motion in all extremities.\nNeurological: Alert and oriented, no focal deficits.\n\nNext Steps:\n- Continue current medication regimen for GERD and kidney stones.\n- Follow-up urinalysis in 6 months to monitor proteinuria.\n- Annual follow-up for kidney stones.\n- Encourage continued healthy lifestyle habits inclu"
